---
// Figure 9: CLIENT TRACKING in its default mode and in broadcast mode.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './cluster.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="cluster-tracking" title="How Redis tells a near cache that a key has changed" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. I sent these commands to Redis 8.10.3 over RESP3 and saw the same invalidation messages; on my laptop each arrived about a quarter of a millisecond after the write. Left out: the near cache's own expiry, and what happens when the connection drops (the near cache must then be emptied).">
  <Choice slot="params" name="mode" label="Tracking mode" value="default"
    options={[{ value: 'default', label: 'default' }, { value: 'bcast', label: 'broadcast (BCAST)' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Default mode: App A reads product:42 and Redis remembers that A's connection read it. When App B writes the key, Redis pushes an invalidation message to A and forgets the key; A drops its copy. B's second write produces no message, because A has not read the key since. A's next read fetches the new value and Redis starts tracking the key for A again.</p>
    <p>Broadcast mode with the prefix product:: Redis keeps no record of A's reads. Every write to a key starting with product: sends A an invalidation, including keys A never read and repeated writes to the same key; writes to other keys, such as order:7, send nothing.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { trackingSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="cluster-tracking"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, trackingSpecs, 'mode'));
</script>
